course: dictionary
lesson: 5153
title: Ironically
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ironically is an adverb, a word that tells us how something happens. It usually means the opposite of what you would expect, and that makes the situation strange or funny. «Ironically, the robbers were caught by a police officer on holiday.» You would expect an officer at work to catch robbers, not one on holiday, so the twist is the irony.
explain: Ironic talk works in a similar way. Someone says something but means the opposite, as a joke. «"Little darling" said ironically» tells you the speaker does not really mean darling: the child is behaving badly, so the words are the reverse of what is true. Look at the clues around the word and ask: does what was said match what really happened?
explain: The clue is the gap between the words and reality. If the words say one thing and the situation shows the reverse, you have probably found irony. Ask yourself: what did I expect, and what actually happened?
fact: Ironically is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens.
fact: Ironically means in a way that is the opposite of what you would expect, which makes it strange or funny.
fact: Ironically can also mean saying something but meaning the opposite, as a joke.
fact: «Ironically, the robbers were caught by a police officer on holiday.» The twist is unexpected, so it is funny.
fact: Irony appears in stories and English lessons, and it often happens in everyday talk too.
exercise: «Ironically, the robbers were caught by a police officer on holiday.» What does ironically show here? | The robbers were very rich | The story is about a lost dog | The officer was angry about his holiday | *The result is the opposite of what you would expect
exercise: «"Little darling" said ironically» means the child was behaving badly. What does ironically tell you about the words "little darling"? | The speaker is talking about a darling cake | *The speaker means the opposite of the words | The speaker is whispering them softly | The speaker is a little child
exercise: Find the word in this sentence that shows the twist: «Ironically, the lifeguard could not swim.» | *Ironically | could | swim | lifeguard
test: «Ironically, the fire station burned down.» What does ironically mean in this sentence? | In a kind and helpful way | Very slowly, with great care | Quickly and loudly | *In a way that is the opposite of what you would expect
test: A friend says «Great, another rainy day» while the sun is shining and rolls their eyes. Why is the friend speaking ironically? | They are telling the truth about the weather | They are talking about a rainy holiday | *They mean the opposite of their words, as a joke | They are happy that it is raining
exam: Ironically is which part of speech? | A noun | *An adverb | A verb | An adjective
